      "message": "Nit: constructs an invalid URL when bmcQuery.data.value is null.\n\nqueryFn can legitimately return null (e.g., empty Members array) without entering an error state. In that case bmcQuery.data.value is null, and:\n\n```\nconst manager \u003d bmcQuery.data.value;  // null\nconst resetTarget \u003d\n  manager?.Actions?.[\u0027#Manager.Reset\u0027]?.target ??\n  `${manager?.[\u0027@odata.id\u0027]}/Actions/Manager.Reset`;\n// → \"undefined/Actions/Manager.Reset\"\n```\n\nAxios resolves this against the base URL, producing a POST to a nonexistent endpoint. The view\u0027s try/catch will surface an error toast, but the root cause is a missing guard, not a real BMC error. The button\u0027s :disabled guard (bmcQuery.isError.value) does not cover the data \u003d\u003d\u003d null case.\n\nRecommendation:\nAdd a guard at the top of mutationFn:\n\n```\nmutationFn: async () \u003d\u003e {\n  const manager \u003d bmcQuery.data.value;\n  if (!manager) throw new Error(\u0027Manager resource not available\u0027);\n  const resetTarget \u003d\n    manager.Actions?.[\u0027#Manager.Reset\u0027]?.target ??\n    `${manager[\u0027@odata.id\u0027]}/Actions/Manager.Reset`;\n  await api.post(resetTarget, { ResetType: \u0027GracefulRestart\u0027 });\n},\n```",
